{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.agents/skills/code-review-pr/SKILL.md\"","aggregated_output":"---\nname: code-review-pr\ndescription: >-\n  Performs comprehensive, high-quality code reviews on Git pull requests, branches, commit ranges, or local working tree diffs. Analyzes code correctness, security, architecture, test coverage, and performance using automated diff analysis and structured review checklists.\n---\n\n# Code Review PR Skill\n\n## Overview\n\nThe `code-review-pr` skill provides an end-to-end framework for conducting rigorous, actionable code reviews on Git pull requests, feature branches, commit ranges, or working tree diffs. It combines automated diff scanning via `scripts/pr_reviewer.py` with structured review checklists across security, correctness, architecture, performance, and testing.\n\nUse this skill whenever asked to:\n- Review a pull request or feature branch (`git diff main...HEAD`).\n- Review recent commits (`git diff HEAD~1` or commit range).\n- Review uncommitted local changes (`git diff` or staged changes).\n- Perform a security, quality, or architecture audit of proposed code changes.\n\n---\n\n## Quick Start\n\nRun the automated PR reviewer script to generate an initial risk assessment, churn metrics, and automated smell scan:\n\n```bash\n# 1. Review branch changes against main (Default PR review)\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target main...HEAD\n\n# 2. Review last commit\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target HEAD~1\n\n# 3. Review uncommitted working tree changes\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target working\n\n# 4. Save review report to file\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target main...HEAD --output review_report.md\n```\n\n---\n\n## Code Review Workflow\n\nFollow this step-by-step process when executing a code review:\n\n### Step 1: Run Automated Diff Analysis\nExecute `scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze` for the target Git ref/range. Review the output to determine:\n- Total lines added/deleted and file churn distribution.\n- High-risk modified files (e.g., Security/Auth, Database/Schema, Infrastructure, API Contracts).\n- Test-to-source additions ratio.\n- Automated static findings (hardcoded secrets, debug leftovers, swallowed exceptions, SQL injection risks).\n\n### Step 2: Deep-Dive Code Inspection\nInspect the diffs for high-risk files using `git diff <target> -- <path>` or `view_file`. Focus on:\n1. **Logic & Edge Cases**:\n   - Boundary checks, null/nil handling, empty collections, zero division.\n   - Resource leaks (unclosed streams, file handles, DB connections).\n   - Race conditions, shared state mutation, concurrency locks.\n2. **Security & Credentials**:\n   - No hardcoded API keys, passwords, or tokens.\n   - Input validation before execution or query interpolation.\n   - Proper authorization checks on new or updated endpoints.\n3. **Architecture & Contracts**:\n   - Preserving backward compatibility on existing API functions/endpoints.\n   - Proper module separation and clean interface boundaries.\n4. **Test Coverage & Quality**:\n   - Ensuring new source logic is accompanied by unit/integration tests.\n   - Verifying tests validate actual assertions rather than just non-crashing execution.\n\n### Step 3: Reference Checklist & Severity Taxonomy\nConsult [references/review_checklist.md](references/review_checklist.md) to classify findings:\n- **P0 (Critical / Blocker)**: Security breaches, data loss risks, severe crashes, hardcoded credentials.\n- **P1 (High / Must Address)**: Logic bugs, broken API contracts, swallowed exceptions, missing critical tests.\n- **P2 (Medium / Recommended)**: Performance bottlenecks, code duplication, missing parameter validation.\n- **P3 (Low / Suggestion)**: Minor formatting, naming improvements, leftover comments/TODOs.\n\n### Step 4: Formulate Structured Review Report\nPresent findings to the user using the following template:\n\n```markdown\n# 🔍 Code Review Report\n\n## Executive Summary\n- **Target:** `<git-ref>`\n- **Verdict:** `[ APPROVED | REQUEST CHANGES | COMMENT ]`\n- **Overall Risk:** `[ LOW | MEDIUM | HIGH ]`\n- **Metrics:** `X files changed, +Y / -Z lines`\n\n## 🚨 Critical Findings (P0 / P1)\n- **[Severity]** `path/to/file.ext:L123` - Short issue description.\n  ```diff\n  - problematic code\n  + suggested fix\n  ```\n\n## 💡 Improvements & Suggestions (P2 / P3)\n- **[Severity]** `path/to/file.ext` - Recommendation details.\n\n## 🧪 Test Coverage & Quality Assessment\n- Evaluation of test ratio and edge case coverage.\n\n## 🌟 Positive Highlights\n- Clean implementations, thorough documentation, or well-structured refactoring.\n```\n\n---\n\n## Utility Script Reference\n\nThe bundled helper script `scripts/pr_reviewer.py` supports the following options:\n\n| Parameter | Type | Default | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `--target` | String | `main...HEAD` | Git range/ref (`main...HEAD`, `HEAD~1`, `staged`, `working`) |\n| `--output` | String | None | File path to write report output |\n| `--format` | String | `markdown` | Output format (`markdown` or `json`) |\n| `--repo-dir` | String | Current Dir | Path to the target Git repository |\n\n---\n\n## Common Review Pitfalls\n\n1. **Nit Tunnel Vision**: Focusing heavily on formatting or naming while missing underlying logic bugs or edge-case crashes.\n2. **Ignoring Test Ratio**: Approving large source code changes without verifying corresponding test coverage additions.\n3. **Swallowing Symptoms**: Suggesting `try/except` wrappers around failing code rather than addressing root cause invalid inputs or broken state.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba public/chp/blog/yin-vm-vs-unison.blog | sed -n '1,360p'; nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn-projection.md | sed -n '1,340p'; rg -n \\\"defn (ast->datoms-with-root|datoms->ast)|yin.code|source|linear|var|load-var|execute|query|q|depend\\\" src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc src/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc src/cljc/dao/space src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc src/cljc/dao/space/query.cljc docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md docs/design/datom.world.md | head -240\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba public/chp/blog/yin-vm-vs-unison.blog | sed -n '1,360p'; nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn-projection.md | sed -n '1,340p'; rg -n \\\"defn (ast->datoms-with-root|datoms->ast)|yin.code|source|linear|var|load-var|execute|query|q|depend\\\" src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc src/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc src/cljc/dao/space src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc src/cljc/dao/space/query.cljc docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md docs/design/datom.world.md | head -240\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t#:blog{:title \"One Truth, Many Interpretations: How yin.vm Solves Unison's Semantic Dilemma\"\n     2\t       :date #inst \"2026-09-21T22:26:54.000+07:00\"\n     3\t       :abstract [:p \"A deep dive into the architectural trade-offs between Unison and yin.vm. Both systems embrace content-addressed code and De Bruijn indexing to solve fundamental distributed programming problems, but they diverge radically on what constitutes the \\\"source of truth\\\", blob stores vs. Datalog tuples, and the true cost of instant refactoring.\"]\n     4\t       :content [:section.blog-article\n     5\t                 [:div.section-inner\n     6\t                  [:article\n     7\t                   :$blog-title\n     8\t                   :$blog-date\n     9\t\n    10\t                   [:p \"Unison and \" [:code \"datom.world\"] \" (via its \" [:code \"yin.vm\"] \" execution engine) share a beautiful, radical premise: source code should not be stored as text files. By treating code as content-addressed data structures, both systems seek to eliminate dependency conflicts, enable trivial codebase distribution, and make global code sharing seamless.\"]\n    11\t                   \n    12\t                   [:p \"To achieve this, both systems rely heavily on a mathematical concept known as \" [:strong \"De Bruijn indexing\"] \" (a technique for representing variable bindings without using names). However, despite these shared foundations, the two systems arrive at completely opposite conclusions about how code should be stored, queried, and evaluated. \"]\n    13\t\n    14\t                   [:h2 \"1. Unison's Radical Simplification: The Nameless AST\"]\n    15\t                   [:p \"In a purely content-addressed system, code is identified by its cryptographic hash. But if you include variable names in that hash, you have a problem. If Developer A writes \" [:code \"(fn [timeout] (+ timeout 1))\"] \" and Developer B writes \" [:code \"(fn [retry] (+ retry 1))\"] \", a naive hashing algorithm sees two completely different functions, breaking deduplication and caching.\"]\n    16\t                   \n    17\t                   [:p \"Unison solves this by making its Abstract Syntax Tree (AST) completely nameless. It strips out local variables and replaces them with De Bruijn indices, and replaces all global function calls with cryptographic hashes. \"]\n    18\t                   \n    19\t                   [:p \"This enables Unison's famous \\\"instant refactoring.\\\" Because the stored code literally has no names, renaming a function or variable doesn't change the AST, meaning the hash never changes. A rename is just updating a UI dictionary pointer, preventing a massive cascading recompilation across the codebase.\"]\n    20\t                   \n    21\t                   [:p \"By making the nameless AST the single source of truth, Unison solved the \\\"wire format dilemma.\\\" When transmitting code across the globe, it transmits purely mathematical, collision-free graphs.\"]\n    22\t\n    23\t                   [:h2 \"2. The Cost: Losing Human Semantics\"]\n    24\t                   [:p \"But Unison pays a heavy price for this simplification. By stripping the names out of the AST, the AST structurally forgets \" [:em \"why\"] \" a variable exists or what it represents. \"]\n    25\t                   \n    26\t                   [:p \"Because Unison's database and VM only understand nameless mathematical nodes, the system loses the original human semantics. It relies entirely on a fragile UI trick where the text editor looks up a hash in a side-dictionary and paints a string onto the screen right before you read it.\"]\n    27\t\n    28\t                   [:h2 \"3. yin.vm: One Truth, Many Interpretations\"]\n    29\t                   [:p \"Instead of throwing away the human semantics, \" [:code \"yin.vm\"] \" embraces a core architectural axiom: \" [:em \"Interpretation Creates Semantics (one truth, many perspectives).\"]]\n    30\t                   \n    31\t                   [:p \"Rather than forcing a single monolithic representation, \" [:code \"yin.vm\"] \" maintains a dual architecture:\"]\n    32\t                   [:ul\n    33\t                    [:li [:strong \"The Named Universal AST: \"] \"This is the unassailable source of truth. It stores the exact human semantics and names the developer wrote. This is what the semantic VM (the ast-walker) executes, allowing stack traces and debuggers to access the real developer intent without needing to reconstruct it from a detached metadata dictionary.\"]\n    34\t                    [:li [:strong \"The De Bruijn Projection: \"] \"This is strictly \" [:em \"derived data\"] \". The system runs an interpreter over the Named AST to project it into a nameless De Bruijn graph solely to calculate the Merkle fingerprint. This provides the mathematical alpha-equivalence needed for caching and deduplication, without mutilating the original semantic source of truth to get there.\"]\n    35\t                   ]\n    36\t                   \n    37\t                   [:p \"When a developer renames a variable in \" [:code \"yin.vm\"] \", they are updating the Named AST. But because the De Bruijn projection absorbs that change and outputs the exact same fingerprint, mathematical identity is preserved.\"]\n    38\t\n    39\t                   [:h2 \"4. The Storage Divide: Blobs vs. Tuples\"]\n    40\t                   [:p \"The most profound difference is how these structures are stored and queried.\"]\n    41\t                   \n    42\t                   [:p \"While Unison uses a SQLite database, it uses it strictly as a Key-Value Blob Store (\" [:code \"Hash -> Serialized Binary Blob\"] \"). To \\\"query\\\" the codebase's structure in Unison, you must write a program that fetches these blobs, deserializes them into memory, and performs programmatic data structure traversal.\"]\n    43\t                   \n    44\t                   [:p \"In \" [:code \"datom.world\"] \", both the Named AST and the De Bruijn projection are stored natively as decomposed relational facts (datoms/tuples). Because every single node and edge is an independent tuple, you don't have to write traversal code. You can write native Datalog queries using \" [:code \"dao.space.query/q\"] \".\"]\n    45\t                   \n    46\t                   [:p \"Want to find all structurally duplicated logic across all modules? You write a Datalog query grouping by the De Bruijn root hash to find mathematical collisions, and simply join back to the Named AST tuples to render the human-readable code blocks that caused them. This level of purely structural querying is impossible when your code is trapped in a serialized blob.\"]\n    47\t\n    48\t                   [:h2 \"Conclusion\"]\n    49\t                   [:p \"Unison treated code purely as mathematical graphs, minimizing architectural complexity at the cost of native human semantics. \" [:code \"yin.vm\"] \" treats code as human semantic data, and pushes the mathematical graphs into a derived projection.\"]\n    50\t                   \n    51\t                   [:p \"By embracing the complexity of maintaining both representations as Datalog-queryable tuples, \" [:code \"yin.vm\"] \" captures the global distribution and deduplication benefits of Unison, without ever losing the human intent behind the code.\"]\n    52\t                   ]]]}\n     1\t---\n     2\tdescription: Alpha-canonical de Bruijn projection of the named Universal AST\n     3\t---\n     4\t\n     5\t# DE BRUIJN PROJECTION OF THE UNIVERSAL AST\n     6\t\n     7\tStatus: design and implementation plan. This is compilation-layer work. The\n     8\tpipeline is:\n     9\t\n    10\t```\n    11\tYang -> named Universal AST -> de Bruijn projection -> projected form\n    12\t                                  |\n    13\t                                  +-> root Merkle fingerprint\n    14\t```\n    15\t\n    16\tThe named form remains the bijective, stored, queryable, and renderable form.\n    17\tThe projected form is derived data for alpha-equivalence, hash identity, and\n    18\tdeduplication. Neither replaces the other.\n    19\t\n    20\t## 1. Architectural position and invariants\n    21\t\n    22\tThe projection is an interpreter over plain AST datoms. It does not execute\n    23\tvalues, primitives, streams, continuations, or effects. It does not change the\n    24\tAST schema, AST walker, semantic VM, linearizer, `dao.stream`, `dao.lease`, or\n    25\tthe waitset.\n    26\t\n    27\tAll state is explicit in the forward-step state: input cursor, current graph\n    28\tframe, indexed facts, scope stack, occurrence memo, pending output, and output\n    29\tcursor. There is no namespace atom, callback, timer, registry, or clock. A\n    30\tblocked input is returned as an outcome; it is never hidden execution.\n    31\t\n    32\tThe input domain is a fully macro-expanded Universal AST. An unexpanded macro\n    33\tcall site is a terminal diagnostic: an application whose operator is a\n    34\t`:lambda` carrying `:macro? true` is unexpanded. A free variable whose name\n    35\tmatches a registry macro cannot be detected from these datoms and is an\n    36\tinherited detection limit. A malformed root, dangling reference, cycle,\n    37\tduplicate structural fact, retract, unknown node type, or unsupported value is\n    38\tlikewise diagnostic rather than silently interpreted.\n    39\t\n    40\t## 2. Input framing and node grammar\n    41\t\n    42\t`yin.vm/ast->datoms-with-root` emits d5 datoms with `:yin/*` attributes. The\n    43\tadapter frames a graph at `:yin/root true`; the marker closes that graph and\n    44\tcauses projection. Exactly one root is required per graph. Several roots are a\n    45\tdiagnostic. End-of-stream with a partial graph is a diagnostic.\n    46\t\n    47\tOnly assert datoms are accepted: `m` must not be the reserved\n    48\t`:db/retract` operation; an assert with other provenance metadata remains\n    49\tvalid. A retract is a diagnostic. Once this validation succeeds, `t` and `m`\n    50\tare excluded from projected identity. Input order and source tempids do not\n    51\taffect identity. The fact index is reset at every root-frame boundary, because\n    52\temitter temporary ids may restart at `-16` for each graph.\n    53\t\n    54\tAttributes in other namespaces, such as `:yin.code/*` or macro-expander event\n    55\tattributes, are ignored. An unknown `:yin/*` attribute on a walked node is a\n    56\tdiagnostic. `:yin/macro-name`, when present, is tolerated as emission metadata\n    57\tand ignored; it is excluded from projected rows and hashes and does not make\n    58\tan unexpanded macro call valid.\n    59\t\n    60\tThe walked node grammar and child order are:\n    61\t\n    62\t| type | scalar/ref attributes | child order |\n    63\t|---|---|---|\n    64\t| `:literal` | `:yin/value` | none |\n    65\t| `:variable` | `:yin/name` | none |\n    66\t| `:lambda` | `:yin/params`, optional `:yin/macro?` | body |\n    67\t| `:application` | operator, operands | operator, operands left-to-right |\n    68\t| `:dao.stream.apply/call` | `:yin/op`, operands | operands left-to-right |\n    69\t| `:if` | test, consequent, alternate | test, consequent, alternate |\n    70\t| `:vm/gensym` | `:yin/prefix` | none |\n    71\t| `:vm/store-get` | `:yin/key` | none |\n    72\t| `:vm/store-put` | `:yin/key`, `:yin/value` | none |\n    73\t| `:vm/current-continuation` | none | none |\n    74\t| `:vm/park` | none | none |\n    75\t| `:vm/resume` | `:yin/parked-id`, `:yin/val-node` | value |\n    76\t| `:stream/make` | `:yin/buffer` | none |\n    77\t| `:stream/put` | `:yin/target`, `:yin/val-node` | target, value |\n    78\t| `:stream/cursor`, `:stream/next`, `:stream/close` | source | source |\n    79\t\n    80\tThe table is exactly the emitter's vocabulary. `:yin/tail?` is derived\n    81\tcontrol-position data: it is ignored, excluded from rows and hashes, and is\n    82\tnot persisted by this projection.\n    83\t\n    84\tThe walk follows fixed child order. A vector is ordered data, never a set. A\n    85\tshared source entity is memoized by `[source-eid lexical-context]`, where\n    86\t`lexical-context` is the complete stack of frame parameter vectors. This memo\n    87\tis only an optimisation; it must never affect output identity. A finite AST\n    88\tgraph is required; cycles are rejected.\n    89\t\n    90\t## 3. Scope and de Bruijn resolution\n    91\t\n    92\tThe scope stack contains ordered parameter vectors. Frame depth `0` is the\n    93\tinnermost frame; position is the source parameter index, with the leftmost\n    94\tparameter at position `0`. Entering a lambda pushes one frame and leaving its\n    95\tbody pops it. The lambda's names are otherwise discarded; its binder becomes\n    96\tan arity.\n    97\t\n    98\tResolution searches frames from inner to outer. Within one frame it searches\n    99\tpositions right-to-left. Thus duplicate parameters have rightmost-wins\n   100\tsemantics, matching VM `bind-params`: `(fn [x x] x)` resolves to the second\n   101\t`x`. A bound occurrence is represented as `{:bound [frame-depth position]}`.\n   102\tAn occurrence with no matching binder is `{:free name}` and preserves its\n   103\tsymbol exactly. An intermediate binder shifts only its own frame depth; the\n   104\tposition pair does not depend on the arity of intervening frames.\n   105\t\n   106\tEvery child is projected under the scope at its occurrence. A lambda body is\n   107\tprojected after its frame is pushed; an application operator and operands share\n   108\tthe enclosing scope.\n   109\t\n   110\t## 4. Projected form and published dimension\n   111\t\n   112\tThe projection publishes a `:yin.debruijn/*` dimension descriptor, as required\n   113\tby `docs/design/datom.md`'s dimension protocol. Its descriptor hash is the\n   114\thash domain separator. A projected semantic node is a content-addressed record\n   115\twhose identity is a 32-byte node hash `h`.\n   116\t\n   117\tThe descriptor declares the projected d5 semantic slots in this order:\n   118\t`[:yin.debruijn/hash :yin.debruijn/type :yin.debruijn/arity\n   119\t:yin.debruijn/bound :yin.debruijn/free :yin.debruijn/value\n   120\t:yin.debruijn/op :yin.debruijn/key :yin.debruijn/prefix\n   121\t:yin.debruijn/buffer :yin.debruijn/parked-id :yin.debruijn/macro?\n   122\t:yin.debruijn/body :yin.debruijn/operator\n   123\t:yin.debruijn/operands :yin.debruijn/test :yin.debruijn/consequent\n   124\t:yin.debruijn/alternate :yin.debruijn/target :yin.debruijn/val-node\n   125\t:yin.debruijn/source :yin.debruijn/root]`. The descriptor also declares each\n   126\tslot's type and the ordered-vector type of `:yin.debruijn/operands`; its hash,\n   127\tnot an ad hoc text string, separates this dimension from every other tuple\n   128\tdimension.\n   129\t\n   130\tThe semantic tuple shape is:\n   131\t\n   132\t```\n   133\t[h tag scalar-slots child-hashes-in-declared-order]\n   134\t```\n   135\t\n   136\tThe d5 storage adapter may use local entity `e` handles, but emits\n   137\t`:yin.debruijn/hash h` on each entity. Local eids, ordinals, row count, root\n   138\tordinal, and the old `-16` base are storage layout only. They are never\n   139\tidentity. Hash-consing equal subterms is the compression mechanism.\n   140\t\n   141\tProjected d5 attributes are:\n   142\t\n   143\t* `:yin.debruijn/type`, `:arity`, `:bound`, and `:free`;\n   144\t* scalar `:value`, `:op`, `:key`, `:prefix`, `:buffer`, `:parked-id`, and\n   145\t  `:macro?`;\n   146\t* child refs `:body`, `:operator`, `:operands`, `:test`, `:consequent`,\n   147\t  `:alternate`, `:target`, `:val-node`, and `:source`.\n   148\t\n   149\tChild refs contain child hashes semantically. `:operands` is an ordered vector\n   150\tof hashes and is never emitted as cardinality-many datoms. The root is an\n   151\texplicit root-hash marker. The named `:yin/name` and `:yin/params` attributes,\n   152\t`:yin/macro-name`, source eids, `:yin/tail?`, and transaction metadata are not\n   153\tprojected into identity. A source-to-projected map is ephemeral by default and, if persisted\n   154\tfor diagnostics, is a separate side index.\n   155\t\n   156\t## 5. Merkle fingerprint and canonical encoding\n   157\t\n   158\tFor every projected node:\n   159\t\n   160\t```\n   161\thash(node) = SHA-256(dimension-hash\n   162\t                     || encode(tag-specific-slots-in-descriptor-order))\n   163\tfingerprint = hash(root)\n   164\t```\n   165\t\n   166\tThe dimension hash follows the d1 content-addressing floor; an ad hoc string\n   167\tdomain separator is not used. Only slots belonging to the node's tag are\n   168\tencoded, in descriptor order. `:yin.debruijn/hash` and\n   169\t`:yin.debruijn/root` are storage/index markers and are excluded from every\n   170\tnode hash. Ordered child hashes preserve operand and branch order. Tree\n   171\temission and graph emission of the same unfolded term therefore have the same\n   172\troot hash; memoisation and sharing cannot change identity.\n   173\t\n   174\tEvery slot is tagged and length-delimited. Keywords and symbols encode\n   175\tnamespace and name separately. Strings use UTF-8 NFC with length prefixes;\n   176\tbytes are length-prefixed; booleans are `00`/`01`; nil has its own tag; maps\n   177\tsort by canonical encoded key bytes; sets sort by canonical encoded element\n   178\tbytes. Lists and vectors are distinct sequence classes and never merge: the\n   179\tlanguage observes the difference, so identity must not. Unsupported values\n   180\tare diagnostics, including an unpaired surrogate and a record literal; a map\n   181\twhose entries collide under key canonicalization is likewise a diagnostic,\n   182\tso iteration order never decides a hash. The preimage is a text stream: each\n   183\tpart frames as its class's tag byte, an 8-hex-digit length, and the content —\n   184\tnumeric and byte content is hex, string content is the raw UTF-8 text, and\n   185\tthe length counts the content's UTF-8 bytes. The hashed bytes are the\n   186\tstream's UTF-8 encoding.\n   187\t\n   188\tNumeric canonicalisation is fixed in D0: exact integers in signed int64 use\n   189\tint64 encoding, and every integral double in that range also uses int64\n   190\tencoding on every host. All other in-domain numbers use IEEE-754 doubles; all\n   191\tNaNs use one quiet-NaN encoding and `+0` and `-0` remain distinct. A\n   192\tJavaScript number is an int64 only when it is a safe integer in the int64 range;\n   193\tan unsafe integer whose exact classification is not recoverable is diagnostic.\n   194\tConsequently, integer `1` and integral double `1.0` intentionally collide in\n   195\tthe canonical form. Bigints, ratios, characters, and other out-of-domain\n   196\tnumeric objects are diagnostic. NFC normalisation collisions, and the\n   197\t`1`/`1.0` collision, are inherited limits of the repository encoding.\n   198\tThe Dart NFC source is settled 2026-09-21: the pure-Dart `unorm_dart`\n   199\tpackage (Unicode 16.0) provides NFC on every ClojureDart target. D3 still\n   200\tproves byte identity on all three hosts against the §8 fixtures.\n   201\t\n   202\t## 6. Stream composition and consumers\n   203\t\n   204\tImplementation belongs in `src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn.cljc`: it consumes the\n   205\texisting `:yin/*` schema and shares no transport code. It exposes:\n   206\t\n   207\t* `project-datoms`: pure projection of one complete rooted graph to Merkle\n   208\t  records and root fingerprint;\n   209\t* `forward-step`: ordinary `dao.stream` forward interpretation. It frames at\n   210\t  the root marker, projects there, emits the projected tuples, and reports a\n   211\t  partial frame at end as diagnostic. Pending writes remain explicit and are\n   212\t  retried only by host cadence.\n   213\t\n   214\tNamed storage, renderers, Datalog queries, source maps, and diagnostics consume\n   215\tthe named stream. Deduplication, cache lookup, and alpha-equivalence consume\n   216\tthe projected segment/fingerprint. The semantic VM and `yin.vm.linearize`\n   217\tcontinue to consume the named AST. A waitset is optional only for a composition\n   218\tthat owns several independent waiters; no lease fact, lease timer, callback,\n   219\tor scheduler is introduced here.\n   220\t\n   221\t## 7. Implementation plan\n   222\t\n   223\t### D0 — contract, dimension, and fixtures\n   224\t\n   225\tPublish the `:yin.debruijn/*` descriptor and hash domain; record the fully\n   226\texpanded macro domain and its lambda-operator detection rule and limit,\n   227\tassert-only input, root framing and per-frame index reset, rightmost duplicate\n   228\tbinding, scope pair convention, canonical value table, NFC and numeric limits,\n   229\tand node grammar. Pin the Dart NFC source (`unorm_dart`, Unicode 16.0)\n   230\tbehind one host-dispatched normalize seam. Add all node fixtures.\n   231\t\n   232\t### D1 — graph index and scope resolver\n   233\t\n   234\tImplement root framing, exact-root validation, unknown-attribute validation,\n   235\tcycle detection, scope push/pop, rightmost duplicate binding, per-frame fact\n   236\tindex reset, and memo keys using the complete frame-vector stack. Completion\n   237\tmeans shuffled input produces the same semantic graph and all bindings resolve\n   238\tto the specified pairs.\n   239\t\n   240\t### D2 — Merkle records and storage adapter\n   241\t\n   242\tImplement node hashes, ordered child hashes, hash-consing, the\n   243\t`:yin.debruijn/*` records, and the d5 storage adapter. Completion means no\n   244\tbinder names, bound occurrence names, tail flags, or source tempids enter\n   245\tidentity, and tree/graph emission has one semantic graph and fingerprint.\n   246\t\n   247\t### D3 — canonical encoder and fingerprint\n   248\t\n   249\tImplement the descriptor-based SHA-256 encoder and numeric/string rules,\n   250\tincluding distinct signed zero and integral-double-to-int64 canonicalisation.\n   251\tCompletion means CLJ, CLJS, and CLJD produce identical bytes and all canonical\n   252\tfixtures hash identically across hosts.\n   253\t\n   254\t### D4 — stream adapter\n   255\t\n   256\tImplement blocked, transport-error, invalid input, pending output, root\n   257\tframing, adjacent graphs (including graphs that reuse temporary eids), and\n   258\tterminal diagnostic outcomes. End-of-stream with a partial graph must be\n   259\tcovered on every host.\n   260\t\n   261\t### D5 — pipeline and storage integration\n   262\t\n   263\tWire Yang's post-emission path before projected persistence. Store named and\n   264\tprojected segments separately; use only the Merkle fingerprint for dedupe.\n   265\tDo not alter named consumers or runtime layers.\n   266\t\n   267\t### D6 — end condition\n   268\t\n   269\tAll D0–D5 criteria pass; alpha-equivalent programs have identical semantic\n   270\tprojected tuples and root hashes; non-equivalent fixtures remain distinct;\n   271\tall emitter types and stream outcomes are covered; and AST, walker, VM,\n   272\tlinearizer, named storage, lease, and waitset diffs are unchanged.\n   273\t\n   274\t## 8. Test matrix\n   275\t\n   276\tThe focused suite must include:\n   277\t\n   278\t* renamed binders at one and multiple nesting levels;\n   279\t* nearest shadowing with `[frame-depth position]` results;\n   280\t* duplicate parameters `(fn [x x] x)` proving rightmost-wins;\n   281\t* free names preserved and changed free names changing the fingerprint;\n   282\t* changed literals, primitive keys, arity, branches, stream operations, and\n   283\t  continuation markers changing the fingerprint;\n   284\t* zero-, one-, and multi-parameter lambdas and parameter order;\n   285\t* every `:vm/*`, `:stream/*`, `:if`, application, and\n   286\t  `:dao.stream.apply/call` emitter case;\n   287\t* shuffled datom input and all three host encoders producing identical bytes;\n   288\t* the same term emitted as a tree and as a graph producing identical Merkle\n   289\t  nodes and root fingerprint;\n   290\t* shared nodes under equal and unequal lexical contexts;\n   291\t* ordered operand changes producing different hashes;\n   292\t* `:yin/tail?` present versus absent producing identical output;\n   293\t* maps and sets with different iteration order producing identical encoding;\n   294\t* `+0.0` and `-0.0` hashing differently;\n   295\t* an integral-double literal hashing identically to its int64 counterpart on\n   296\t  every host;\n   297\t* dangling refs, duplicate facts, cycles, missing/multiple roots, unknown\n   298\t  node types, unknown `:yin/*` attributes, retracts, unexpanded macros,\n   299\t  unsupported values, and partial frames at end producing diagnostics;\n   300\t* adjacent rooted graphs in one input stream that reuse emitter eids beginning\n   301\t  at `-16`;\n   302\t* numeric boundary, NaN, signed-zero, JS-number, and NFC-limit fixtures;\n   303\t* blocked, transport-error, invalid-answer, and pending-write paths;\n   304\t* the essay's `(fn [count] (+ count 1))` example and stable fingerprint;\n   305\t* unchanged named AST reconstruction, linearization, semantic execution,\n   306\t  lease, and waitset suites.\n   307\t\n   308\t## 9. What must not change\n   309\t\n   310\tThe `:yin/*` schema, emitter, `datoms->ast`, walker, VM protocols, semantic\n   311\tmachine, `yin.vm.linearize`, `:yin.code/*`, bijective named storage,\n   312\t`dao.stream`, transports, `dao.lease`, and `dao.stream.waitset` remain outside\n   313\tthis design's authority. The only new pressure is a published projected\n   314\tdimension and optional diagnostic side index; neither changes the named form.\n   315\t\n   316\t## 10. Resolved owner decisions\n   317\t\n   318\tThe six open questions are resolved: source-leftmost parameter position is\n   319\tzero; duplicate names are rightmost-wins; shared nodes may be duplicated by\n   320\tlexical context but sharing is invisible to identity; maps and sets use\n   321\tcanonical byte ordering; the published `:yin.debruijn/*` dimension supplies\n   322\tthe domain separator and SHA-256 is the hash; the source-to-projected index is\n   323\tephemeral by default and separate if later persisted. The Dart NFC source is\n   324\t`unorm_dart` (Unicode 16.0), settled by the owner 2026-09-21 and validated by\n   325\tthe D3 cross-host fixtures. At the D0 review 2026-09-21 the value table's\n   326\tsequence class was ruled split: lists and vectors are distinct classes and\n   327\tnever merge, because a program can observe the difference and identity never\n   328\tmerges distinguishable values.\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc:1:(ns yin.vm.linearize\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc:2:  \"Lowering: Universal AST datoms (`:yin/*`) → linear executable datoms\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc:3:   (`:yin.code/*`), per `docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md` §5.\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc:7:   right), emitting the main sequence first and lambda bodies out of line\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc:14:   `:yin.code/source` and reconstructed maps have no entities.\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc:23:  (:require [dao.datom :as datom]\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc:36:  #{:yin.code/target :yin.code/body})\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc:54:  [source attr v]\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc:57:                    {:node source, :attr attr, :value v}))))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc:62:   vector of `{:op :source :operands [[attr v] ..]}` in pc order, and `labels`\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc:71:        emit! (fn [source op & operands]\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc:73:                  (doseq [[a v] operands\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc:75:                    (reject-host-value! source a v))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc:77:                         {:op op, :source source, :operands operands})))]\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc:84:             :literal (emit! e :const :yin.code/value (get-attr e :yin/value))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc:85:             :variable (emit! e :var :yin.code/name (get-attr e :yin/name))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc:89:                              :yin.code/params (get-attr e :yin/params)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc:90:                              :yin.code/body l))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc:94:                            (doseq [o operands]\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc:98:                                   :yin.code/argc (count operands)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc:99:                                   :yin.code/tail? (boolean\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc:104:                   (emit! e :branch-false :yin.code/target else)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc:105:                   (lower-node (get-attr e :yin/consequent))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc:106:                   (emit! e :jump :yin.code/target end)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc:111:                                      (doseq [o operands]\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc:115:                                             :yin.code/ffi-op (get-attr e :yin/op)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc:116:                                             :yin.code/argc (count operands)))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc:118:           