{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc | sed -n '1,180p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc | sed -n '40,75p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc | sed -n '552,625p'; nl -ba docs/design/datom.world.md | sed -n '1,150p'; nl -ba docs/orchestrator-log.md | tail -120\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t(ns yin.vm\n     2\t  \"Canonical data model (Datoms), schema, and primitives for the Yin Abstract\n     3\t   Machine on DaoStream v2.\n     4\t\n     5\t   The shared kernel of `yin.vm` carries over unchanged. What changes is the\n     6\t   stream wiring:\n     7\t\n     8\t   - **The host supplies streams.** v1 hardcoded a ring buffer descriptor and\n     9\t     called `ds/open!`. Here a composition hands the interpreter a\n    10\t     `:make-stream` constructor exactly as it hands it `:primitives`. There is\n    11\t     no default, because a default would smuggle the hardcoded transport back\n    12\t     in: absent a supplied constructor, `:stream/make` is unsupported and says\n    13\t     so.\n    14\t   - **Program observation lives outside the VM.** The program stream handle,\n    15\t     cursor, and gap count belong to `dao.stream.observer`; the VM never\n    16\t     polls a program stream and no longer accepts `:in-stream` at\n    17\t     construction. Language-level stream effects and FFI still operate their\n    18\t     own streams and cursors inside the VM.\n    19\t   - **Cursors are opaque.** The VM's internal cursor representation is\n    20\t     `{:stream-id id :cursor <opaque>}`. There is no position and no seek.\n    21\t   - **Construction is all-or-nothing.** Creating the FFI pair and minting its\n    22\t     cursors are stream operations with their own outcomes. Any non-`ok`\n    23\t     outcome fails construction; a VM cannot be half-built.\n    24\t   - **`call-in-cursor-key` is dropped.** v1 wrote it and nothing read it.\"\n    25\t  (:refer-clojure :exclude [eval])\n    26\t  (:require [dao.datom :as datom]\n    27\t            [dao.jing :as jing]\n    28\t            [dao.space.query :as query]\n    29\t            [dao.stream :as stream]))\n    30\t\n    31\t\n    32\t;; =============================================================================\n    33\t;; Protocols\n    34\t;; =============================================================================\n    35\t;; Common VM interfaces implemented by all execution engines.\n    36\t\n    37\t(defprotocol IVM\n    38\t  \"Unified VM protocol for execution, evaluation, and observable state.\"\n    39\t\n    40\t  (step\n    41\t    [vm]\n    42\t    \"Execute one step of already-loaded work. An idle VM is returned\n    43\t     unchanged; program input arrives through an attached stream observer.\")\n    44\t\n    45\t  (run\n    46\t    [vm]\n    47\t    \"Run already-loaded work until halted or blocked.\")\n    48\t\n    49\t  (eval\n    50\t    [vm ast]\n    51\t    \"Convert and evaluate a supplied AST. Does not drain any independently\n    52\t     queued program input; explicit observer coordination does that.\")\n    53\t\n    54\t  (reset\n    55\t    [vm]\n    56\t    \"Reset execution state to a known initial state, preserving loaded program.\")\n    57\t\n    58\t  (halted?\n    59\t    [vm]\n    60\t    \"Returns true if VM has halted (completed or error).\")\n    61\t\n    62\t  (blocked?\n    63\t    [vm]\n    64\t    \"Returns true if VM is blocked waiting for external input.\")\n    65\t\n    66\t  (value\n    67\t    [vm]\n    68\t    \"Returns the current result value, or nil if not yet computed.\"))\n    69\t\n    70\t\n    71\t(defprotocol IVMState\n    72\t  \"CESK state accessor protocol.\n    73\t   Exposes the four components of the CESK machine model.\n    74\t   Representations are VM-specific but the structure is universal.\"\n    75\t\n    76\t  (control\n    77\t    [vm]\n    78\t    \"Returns the current control state (what is being evaluated).\n    79\t     VM-specific: AST node, instruction pointer, node ID, etc.\")\n    80\t\n    81\t  (environment\n    82\t    [vm]\n    83\t    \"Returns the current lexical environment (variable bindings).\")\n    84\t\n    85\t  (store\n    86\t    [vm]\n    87\t    \"Returns the current store (heap/global state).\")\n    88\t\n    89\t  (continuation\n    90\t    [vm]\n    91\t    \"Returns the current continuation (what to do next).\n    92\t     VM-specific: linked frames, stack vector, call-stack, etc.\"))\n    93\t\n    94\t\n    95\t;; Primitive operations\n    96\t;; Arithmetic and comparison ops are direct clojure.core references.\n    97\t;; Wrapped only where VM semantics require it:\n    98\t;; - rest: clojure.core/rest returns a lazy seq; wrapped to return a vector\n    99\t;;   so that conj appends rather than prepends.\n   100\t;; - /: only the JVM throws on an integral zero divisor; JS and Dart divide\n   101\t;;   IEEE-754 and yield Infinity, so checked-divide makes the error\n   102\t;;   host-uniform.\n   103\t;; - yin/def, require: return effect descriptors consumed by the engine.\n   104\t(defn- checked-divide\n   105\t  \"clojure.core `/` except a zero divisor throws on every host.\n   106\t\n   107\t   JVM `/` throws `Divide by zero` for integral division only; JS and Dart\n   108\t   yield Infinity, so `(/ 1 0)` through the VM silently evaluated to `##Inf`\n   109\t   on those hosts. The REPL corpus pins divide-by-zero as an error with this\n   110\t   text on both evaluators (docs/design/yin.vm.divergence-register.md).\n   111\t   JS and Dart cannot distinguish `0` from `0.0`, so no rule reproduces the\n   112\t   JVM's double-division `##Inf` cross-host; the uniform error is the\n   113\t   contract.\"\n   114\t  ([x] (checked-divide 1 x))\n   115\t  ([x y]\n   116\t   (when (and (number? y) (zero? y))\n   117\t     (throw (ex-info \"Divide by zero\" {:divisor y})))\n   118\t   (/ x y))\n   119\t  ([x y & more] (reduce checked-divide (checked-divide x y) more)))\n   120\t\n   121\t\n   122\t(defn primitive-profile\n   123\t  \"Build UCF §7.5.2's published profile record for `name`.\n   124\t\n   125\t   The address covers the name and all four semantic declarations. The name\n   126\t   remains the registry key rather than being duplicated in the returned\n   127\t   record. Profile values contain no host function objects and are therefore\n   128\t   ordinary portable data.\"\n   129\t  [name class arities effects host-state]\n   130\t  (let [description {:yin.k/name name,\n   131\t                     :yin.k/class class,\n   132\t                     :yin.k/arities (vec arities),\n   133\t                     :yin.k/effects (set effects),\n   134\t                     :yin.k/host-state host-state}]\n   135\t    {:yin.k/profile (keyword \"yin.k.pp\"\n   136\t                             (str \"sha256-\" (jing/content-hash description))),\n   137\t     :yin.k/class class,\n   138\t     :yin.k/arities (vec arities),\n   139\t     :yin.k/effects (set effects),\n   140\t     :yin.k/host-state host-state}))\n   141\t\n   142\t\n   143\t(defn primitive-function\n   144\t  \"Return the executable value published by a primitive registry entry.\n   145\t\n   146\t   Bare entries remain supported for composition-supplied legacy registries.\"\n   147\t  [entry]\n   148\t  (if (and (map? entry) (contains? entry :yin.k/function))\n   149\t    (:yin.k/function entry)\n   150\t    entry))\n   151\t\n   152\t\n   153\t(defn- primitive-entry\n   154\t  [name function class arities effects]\n   155\t  (assoc (primitive-profile name class arities effects :none)\n   156\t         :yin.k/function function))\n   157\t\n   158\t\n   159\t(def primitives\n   160\t  \"The standard executable primitive registry, published with UCF §7.5.2\n   161\t   profiles. `:yin.k/function` is host-local; the five profile keys are the\n   162\t   portable publication. `:variadic` means the greatest listed fixed arity\n   163\t   extends to further arguments.\"\n   164\t  (let [pure (fn [name function arities]\n   165\t               [name (primitive-entry name function :pure arities #{})])]\n   166\t    (into {}\n   167\t          [(pure '+ + [0 1 2 :variadic])\n   168\t           (pure '- - [1 2 :variadic])\n   169\t           (pure '* * [0 1 2 :variadic])\n   170\t           (pure '/ checked-divide [1 2 :variadic])\n   171\t           (pure '= = [0 1 2 :variadic])\n   172\t           (pure '== = [0 1 2 :variadic])\n   173\t           (pure '!= not= [1 2 :variadic])\n   174\t           (pure '< < [1 2 :variadic])\n   175\t           (pure '> > [1 2 :variadic])\n   176\t           (pure '<= <= [1 2 :variadic])\n   177\t           (pure '>= >= [1 2 :variadic])\n   178\t           (pure 'not not [1])\n   179\t           (pure 'nil? nil? [1])\n   180\t           (pure 'empty? empty? [1])\n    40\t\n    41\t(defn- fail\n    42\t  [message data]\n    43\t  (throw (ex-info message data)))\n    44\t\n    45\t\n    46\t(defn bind-params\n    47\t  \"Zips params with args, nil-filling any params beyond args' length.\n    48\t   Extra args beyond params' length are dropped. §7.7.2: an under-arity\n    49\t   call leaves missing parameter names bound to nil, not absent.\"\n    50\t  [params args]\n    51\t  (into {} (map vector params (concat args (repeat nil)))))\n    52\t\n    53\t\n    54\t(defn resolve-var\n    55\t  \"Look up a variable name: env -> store -> primitives -> module registry.\n    56\t\n    57\t   The registry is a value supplied by the composition, not a global.\"\n    58\t  [env store primitives registry name]\n    59\t  (if-let [pair (find env name)]\n    60\t    (val pair)\n    61\t    (if-let [pair (find store name)]\n    62\t      (val pair)\n    63\t      (if-let [pair (find primitives name)]\n    64\t        (vm/primitive-function (val pair))\n    65\t        (if-let [resolved (when (namespace name)\n    66\t                            (module/resolve-module\n    67\t                              registry\n    68\t                              (symbol (str (namespace name)\n    69\t                                           \".\"\n    70\t                                           (clojure.core/name name)))))]\n    71\t          resolved\n    72\t          (fail (str \"Unable to resolve symbol: \" name \" in this context\")\n    73\t                {:symbol name}))))))\n    74\t\n    75\t\n   552\t(defn build-program-index\n   553\t  [datoms]\n   554\t  (group-by first (vec datoms)))\n   555\t\n   556\t\n   557\t(defn frame-versions\n   558\t  [frame]\n   559\t  (loop [f frame\n   560\t         acc #{}]\n   561\t    (if (map? f)\n   562\t      (let [acc (cond-> acc (:compiled-version f) (conj (:compiled-version f)))]\n   563\t        (recur (:next f) acc))\n   564\t      acc)))\n   565\t\n   566\t\n   567\t(defn collect-frame-versions\n   568\t  [frames]\n   569\t  (reduce (fn [acc f] (into acc (frame-versions f))) #{} (or frames [])))\n   570\t\n   571\t\n   572\t(defn pinned-compiled-versions\n   573\t  [vm]\n   574\t  (let [active (:active-compiled-version vm)\n   575\t        k-versions (frame-versions (:k vm))\n   576\t        parked-versions (collect-frame-versions (vals (:parked vm)))\n   577\t        ready-versions (collect-frame-versions (:ready-queue vm))\n   578\t        wait-versions (collect-frame-versions (:wait-set vm))]\n   579\t    (cond-> (clojure.set/union k-versions\n   580\t                               parked-versions\n   581\t                               ready-versions\n   582\t                               wait-versions)\n   583\t      active (conj active))))\n   584\t\n   585\t\n   586\t(defn trim-compiled-cache\n   587\t  [vm]\n   588\t  (let [cache (:compiled-by-version vm)\n   589\t        limit\n   590\t        (max 1 (or (:compiled-cache-limit vm) default-compiled-cache-limit))]\n   591\t    (if (> (count cache) limit)\n   592\t      (let [pinned (pinned-compiled-versions vm)\n   593\t            sorted-versions (reverse (sort (keys cache)))\n   594\t            to-keep (into pinned (take limit sorted-versions))]\n   595\t        (assoc vm :compiled-by-version (select-keys cache to-keep)))\n   596\t      vm)))\n   597\t\n   598\t\n   599\t(defn cache-compiled-artifact\n   600\t  [vm version artifact program-index]\n   601\t  (-> vm\n   602\t      (assoc :compile-dirty? false\n   603\t             :datom-index program-index\n   604\t             :active-compiled-version version)\n   605\t      (update :compiled-by-version (fnil assoc {}) version artifact)\n   606\t      trim-compiled-cache))\n   607\t\n   608\t\n   609\t(defn ensure-compiled-version\n   610\t  [vm version compile-fn]\n   611\t  (if-let [artifact (get-in vm [:compiled-by-version version])]\n   612\t    [vm artifact]\n   613\t    (let [program-datoms (vec (or (:datoms vm) []))\n   614\t          program-root-eid (:program-root-eid vm)]\n   615\t      (when (or (empty? program-datoms) (nil? program-root-eid))\n   616\t        (fail \"Canonical program is not loaded\" {:program-version version}))\n   617\t      (let [program-index (or (:datom-index vm)\n   618\t                              (build-program-index program-datoms))\n   619\t            artifact (compile-fn program-root-eid program-datoms program-index)\n   620\t            vm' (cache-compiled-artifact vm version artifact program-index)]\n   621\t        [vm' artifact]))))\n   622\t\n   623\t\n   624\t(defn maybe-recompile-at-boundary\n   625\t  [vm compile-fn]\n     1\t---\n     2\tdescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\n     3\t---\n     4\t\n     5\t# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\n     6\t\n     7\t## Core Philosophy\n     8\t\n     9\tEverything is data.\n    10\tInterpretation > abstraction.\n    11\tExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\n    12\tRestrictions are a feature.\n    13\t\n    14\t### Foundational Axioms\n    15\t\n    16\t1. **Everything is a Stream**: All IO and data flow through append-only streams.\n    17\t2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\n    18\t3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\n    19\t4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\n    20\t\n    21\t## Non-Negotiable Invariants\n    22\t\n    23\t- Do not introduce hidden global state.\n    24\t- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\n    25\t- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\n    26\t- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\n    27\t- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\n    28\t- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\n    29\t\n    30\t## Tuples and Datoms\n    31\t\n    32\tTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\n    33\tA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\n    34\tSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\n    35\t\n    36\tSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\n    37\t\n    38\t## Streams\n    39\t\n    40\tAll IO is modeled as a stream. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\n    41\tNo direct function-to-function coupling without a stream boundary.\n    42\tSide effects must appear as stream emissions.\n    43\tStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\n    44\tStreams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\n    45\t\n    46\tA stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\n    47\tof interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\n    48\tsemantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\n    49\tstream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\n    50\tthe very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\n    51\tanswers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\n    52\texists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\n    53\tthe other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\n    54\tand the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\n    55\tbecomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\n    56\tthe stream's own.\n    57\t\n    58\tFunctions can take anything and return anything.\n    59\tAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Agent behavior is specified entirely through stream effects.\n    60\t\n    61\tSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\n    62\tSee [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\n    63\t\n    64\t### Host Boundaries\n    65\t\n    66\tA host boundary is a stream boundary, never a function call. The one\n    67\tsanctioned function-shaped contact is a `dao.stream` handle operation, whose\n    68\texhaustive outcome map is stream protocol, not an application callback.\n    69\t\n    70\t**Effects out.** Portable code appends an effect value describing what must\n    71\thappen. A host interpreter consumes that stream, performs the operation, and\n    72\tappends the outcome. Portable code reads the outcome. Correlation is by\n    73\tidentity in the data; neither side holds a reference to the other. A host with\n    74\tno implementation for an effect emits a qualified unsupported result. That is a\n    75\tcorrect outcome, not a gap to be filled.\n    76\t\n    77\t**Events in.** When the host initiates (a socket receives, a connection drops,\n    78\ta timer fires) the adapter appends one plain-data event and returns. It invokes\n    79\tnothing. An interpreter reads that stream and decides what the event means.\n    80\tConstructively: **a callback is replaced by a function that transforms an event\n    81\tto data on a stream.** The host's demand for a callback stops at that function.\n    82\t\n    83\t**Adapters.** An adapter is a map of those transform functions. That is all an\n    84\tadapter is. It is a value, not a namespace, and it is general: sockets, files,\n    85\ttimers and windows all deliver events by calling something, and an adapter is\n    86\twhat they call. Each entry takes the host's arguments and returns one value;\n    87\tthe append is the adapter's, not the entry's, so an entry cannot return a value\n    88\tto the host and cannot invoke application code. The adapter is handed only the\n    89\tdeposit operation and its own host resource. If its deposit channel fails, it\n    90\tmay close that resource to make the boundary observably gone; this is teardown,\n    91\tnot an upward callback.\n    92\t\n    93\tHost types are classified into plain data in the transform: it is the last\n    94\tplace holding a host error object, and no host type crosses onto the stream. A\n    95\ttransport may use a qualified envelope to preserve correlation, lifecycle, and\n    96\tadditive evolution; that envelope remains plain data and creates no separate\n    97\tevent mechanism. A composition may likewise define its own qualified vocabulary\n    98\tfor facts it owns.\n    99\t\n   100\tWhich stream an adapter writes to is the caller's composition, never the\n   101\tadapter's knowledge. An adapter cannot tell whether it deposits into one\n   102\ttransport's private stream or into a medium many interpreters read. This keeps\n   103\tthe layering one-way: a boundary depends on nothing above it, because whoever\n   104\tconstructs the adapter supplies the stream.\n   105\t\n   106\tA transform deposits every event admitted by the boundary protocol and judges\n   107\tnone of them semantically. Wire framing, decoding, and protocol validation\n   108\thappen below the transform: malformed wire input is transport machinery, not an\n   109\tevent. Once the transport has produced a valid host event, data is syntax\n   110\t(axiom 2): an event that is noise to one interpreter is signal to another that\n   111\tdoes not exist yet, and filtering at the depositing layer destroys perspectives\n   112\tnot yet taken. Retention is the medium's operational policy; meaning is\n   113\tinterpretation. Neither is the depositor's decision.\n   114\t\n   115\tTwo things are not adapters. One that exposes a function for portable code to\n   116\tcall isolates the host library but keeps the coupling, and the effect never\n   117\tappears as an emission. One that takes a function to invoke has relocated the\n   118\tcallback, not removed it, even when every value it passes is plain data; if\n   119\tthat callback has a meaningful return value, it is a synchronous call in both\n   120\tdirections wearing the shape of an event.\n   121\t\n   122\t## Architectural Commitments\n   123\t\n   124\t- **`dao.jing` is syntax, agents are semantics**: the content-addressed encoding stays passive and payload-agnostic.\n   125\t- **Macros are stream topology**: an evaluator knows nothing about macro expansion; expansion happens on the syntax side of a medium boundary, upstream of any evaluator.\n   126\t- **Peer observers, one stream**: a generic evaluator and `dao.space` independently observe the same stream topic, as described under Streams. This is not a claim about any two consumers regardless of what they consume.\n   127\t- **Observers are independent and optional**: nothing requires a medium to have any particular set of consumers. Which observers exist for a stream is a per-composition choice, not a design-time commitment.\n   128\t\n   129\t## Design Principles\n   130\t\n   131\tCode quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?\n   132\tStigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.\n   133\t\n   134\t**Derive, don't persist.** Before adding a tag, slot, or field to any\n   135\tcanonical content-hashed structure (an AST grammar, a datom schema, a row\n   136\tshape), check whether a correctly scoped Datalog query over\n   137\t`dao.space.query/q` can already compute the fact. If it can, it belongs as a\n   138\tquery or projection: new persisted structure migrates every downstream\n   139\tconsumer and duplicates authority over a fact a query derives fresh. The\n   140\tretired `:global` tag in\n   141\t[`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md) is the\n   142\tcase study, including the scoping bug that a naive query introduced.\n   143\t\n   144\tSee [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).\n   145\tSee [`docs/agents/vocabulary.md`](../agents/vocabulary.md) for domain vocabulary reference (biology, economics, physics, philosophy).\n   146\t\n   147\t## Code Style\n   148\t\n   149\t- Prefer simple data over rich types.\n   150\t- Prefer total functions over partial ones.\n  5756\tSession-ID: not-applicable (interactive seat)\n  5757\tTree: unchanged (master@286f9e2c)\n  5758\tDone: The previous entry's header reads 20:32:00 +07 (Completed-GMT 13:32:00); the actual append time was 2026-09-20 17:26:27 +07 (Completed-GMT 2026-09-20 10:26:27 GMT). Third occurrence of the same error class. The prior entry's claimed process fix (\"capture the real time at write\") failed because the header was still composed before the date command ran in the same shell invocation. The structural fix now in force: the seat runs `date` in its own tool call, reads the output, and only then composes the entry with that value pasted — never drafting header text in advance. All other fields of the previous entry are accurate.\n  5759\tDecisions: Correction as a new entry per append-only protocol; header timestamps henceforth copied from a separately-captured date output only.\n  5760\tVerification: The date command output printed directly above the appended entry (17:26:27 +07).\n  5761\tDelegates: none\n  5762\tNext: unchanged from the corrected entry.\n  5763\t## 2026-09-21 01:02:15 +07 — evening docs session: contract rulings, plan-rule promotion, site characters, blog rewrites\n  5764\tCompleted-GMT: 2026-09-20 18:02:15 GMT\n  5765\tCoding-Agent: interactive\n  5766\tSession-ID: not-applicable (interactive seat)\n  5767\tTree: master@ca04467f committed (all units below); untracked: docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn-projection.md (de Bruijn unit still in review, separate entry to follow); origin/master is 2 commits behind (75cae8f9, ca04467f)\n  5768\tDone: Four interactive units at the user's direction, all committed and (except the last two blog commits) pushed. (1) dao.lease contract: the user's grid-table reflow committed verbatim as 775937e0; the two-architect contract mob (gpt-5.6-sol × fable-5-1, three rounds) ruled the open §6 questions and the rulings landed as 8d14ffda — Q1: an unknown lease is due for :silence only when BOTH the known bound AND a full duration since the resumed reading hold (a gap may delay a reclaim, never speed one up); Q2: the holder's cap basis is its observed grant and fencing, not tolerance, covers the judge/holder flight-time gap; Q3: the waitset plan's tentative latency note replaced with the accepted composition-policy wording. The four Q1-adjacent plan-only rules (truncated-drain silence suppression, the 2^52 exactness bound, fail-closed unusable readings, sizing strict-half) were promoted from the transient plan into dao.lease.md as 7f5c21ef so the plan can be retired without loss. (2) Site characters/icons (a5843ee0, 627b154a, 660e9a9a, fbba6785, c8baa576, 4e648d63, df0586fb, f0efd65c, 04f6dae9): each dao-* page gained an h1 Chinese character — 井 confirmed for DaoJing by dao-jing.chp's own text, 租 for DaoLease, 道空 for DaoSpace, 道 leading every dao-* h1; the DaoLease page itself (借道 h1, four-cause table, anti-spoofing section, JIT+GC essay link) landed in this batch on master after the user's branch-history rework folded the earlier ada8ea20 lineage in. (3) Blog rewrites for the tuple era (613dd7d6, 75cae8f9, ca04467f): universal-ast-vs-assembly rewritten around semantic tuples rather than datoms, the projection-as-tuples post, and the content-addressed index-segments post — aligning the public prose with the de Bruijn/segment design thread.\n  5769\tDecisions: The user asked how a Chinese reader would read the character compounds; the resolution was to lead h1s with 道 alone (the X道 discipline-of-the-thing reading, as in 茶道/剑道) instead of compounds that read oddly in Chinese. Plan→contract promotion keeps the contract operative and the plan deletable. Q2 was accepted rather than engineered away: the mob judged fencing the right mechanism, so no tolerance was added.\n  5770\tVerification: Commits verified by git log/show at append time; no source files touched (docs/site only), so no test lanes run. The pushed/unpushed boundary re-derived from `git log origin/master..master` immediately before this entry: 75cae8f9 and ca04467f await the user's push word.\n  5771\tDelegates: gpt-5.6-sol and claude-fable-5-1 as the contract mob (sessions 01a0bacb… and 8fafe5c6…, three rounds each, artifacts under collab/ with earlier prefixes)\n  5772\tNext: The de Bruijn design unit (separate entry): reviewer confirmation returned 4 P2 + 3 P3 residual single-sentence edits with pre-authorized closure; architect r3 applying them now. Then: commit the design doc, user's morning design review, implementation dispatch. Also open: push for the two blog commits, worktree/branch cleanup (debruijn-projection worktree has no unique commits), compiler-engineer role-file rename note.\n  5773\t## 2026-09-21 01:05:56 +07 — de Bruijn projection design: authored, review-closed, committed\n  5774\tCompleted-GMT: 2026-09-20 18:05:56 GMT\n  5775\tCoding-Agent: interactive\n  5776\tSession-ID: not-applicable (interactive seat)\n  5777\tTree: master@efab4fa2 committed (docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn-projection.md, 312 lines); uncommitted: this log; origin/master 3 commits behind (75cae8f9, ca04467f, efab4fa2)\n  5778\tDone: The canonical-code-form design the user commissioned (\"let's redesign yin.vm around the Universal AST and alpha equivalence\") is authored, review-closed and on master. Authoring went to the gpt-5.6-sol architect thread 01a0bacb (resumed from the consensus/waitset sign-off thread) after a glm-5.3-flash storage-engineer draft dispatch was rerouted per the user's correction — the design-spec stage is architect work. Adversarial review (claude-fable-5-1, resumed lease/waitset session 8fafe5c6) returned 2 P1 + 7 P2: duplicate parameters resolved to the wrong binder vs bind-params' rightmost-wins, and identity depending on emission history (node sharing/ordinals) — the architect's r2 replaced ordinal hashing with Merkle node hashing and applied all nine. Confirmation round r2 (same reviewer) resolved eight of nine and returned seven residual single-sentence edits (R1 root marker inside the hashed slots; R2 :yin/macro-name is emission history like :yin/tail?; R3 signed zeros must be distinct; R4 integral doubles need a host-stable ruling; R5-R7 P3 detection-rule/assert-test/frame-index wording). Architect r3 applied all seven as written, adopting the reviewer's recommended R4 option (integral doubles in int64 range canonicalize as int64 on every host; the 1/1.0 collision is documented beside the NFC limit), and the reviewer had pre-authorized closure in that case — no further review round. The doc specifies: fully macro-expanded input, assert-only batches, exactly one root, rightmost-wins duplicate binding, Merkle identity over tag-specific slots in descriptor order, the :yin.debruijn/* dimension with descriptor-hash domain separator, numeric canonicalization, D0-D6 phases, and a §8 matrix including (fn [x x] x), tree-vs-graph emission, ±0.0, integral doubles, and adjacent graphs reusing -16-based eids.\n  5779\tDecisions: Reviewer budget conserved by closing per its own pre-authorization instead of a third fable round (fable at ~17% until Tuesday 04:00). The codex -s-after-resume flag error recurred once and was fixed by parent-level placement (codex exec -s workspace-write resume <id>); the failed invocation is the first 178 bytes of the r3 log. R4 was delegated to the architect with the reviewer's recommendation as default and a deviation duty; no deviation occurred.\n  5780\tVerification: R1-R7 spot-checked in the committed doc by grep (tag-specific slot hash formula at line 162, macro-name exclusion at 56/152, distinct zeros at 183, integral-double int64 at 181, macro detection at 34, retract-only rejection at 48, frame-boundary index reset at 51) plus the r3 per-finding confirmation in collab/1789927255888-architect-debruijn-design-r3.gpt-5.6-sol.stdout.log (thread 01a0bacb). Docs-only unit: no test lanes run.\n  5781\tDelegates: gpt-5.6-sol (01a0bacb…, authoring r1/r2/r3; artifacts 1789925319000-*, 1789927000000-*-r2, 1789927255888-*-r3), claude-fable-5-1 (8fafe5c6…, review + confirmation; artifacts 1789927000000-reviewer-*, 1789927110533-*-r2), glm-5.3-flash (rerouted draft dispatch, 1789924845000-*)\n  5782\tNext: HELD FOR THE USER: design review of docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn-projection.md (they said they would read it on waking); on gre