ocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:415:in `ast_walker.cljc` today; Phase 0 lifts both into `yin.vm.ffi` so that\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:424:are `{:segment :pc :env :stack :k :reason :cursor-ref/:stream-id}` — pure\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:427:round-trip (§1.1's serialization limit; the program's own `env`/stack values\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:433:                                       env, stack, k, val from entry\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:437:`engine/resume-continuation`, exactly as `ast-walker-restore` is;\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:444:### 3.6 Observation and traces\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:447:observation the design reserves a trace vocabulary emitted at park points\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:451:[cfg :yin.trace/segment seg t] [cfg :yin.trace/pc pc t] [cfg :yin.trace/val v t]\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:452:[cfg :yin.trace/env-ref e t]   [cfg :yin.trace/k-depth d t]\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:457:`as-of` and causal debugging are queries over a stream, not interpretation\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:459:trace lands there when telemetry is designed, and nothing in Phase 1–3\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:473:  `val`, and the operand stack `St` (a vector; `St` is control, not store,\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:478:  walker does. `bind-params` binds each param to its positional argument and\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:489:  `{:type :return :segment :pc :env :stack-base}` for calls, and the\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:492:  `:segment :pc :stack`.\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:523:A tail call grows neither $K$ nor $St$: the walker gets this for free\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:561:Compare the walker's evaluation of one operand: it allocates a frame map,\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:588:walker consumes; the implementation may reconstruct maps with\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:640:Evaluation order is the walker's (operator, then operands left to right),\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:646:Every instruction gets `:yin.code/source` = the AST node eid it came from,\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:648:direction (render an instruction range back to source) is a query, not a\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:684:For `(+ x 1)` the walker performs roughly nine allocations and a dozen map\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:711:path at all — it is paid once at load. The historic 1.0–1.1x register/stack\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:712:line in `cesk-space-optimization.md` is the target; the walker sits at\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:722:- **Host-array operand stack** inside the hot loop, materialized to a vector\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:751:| Mean time per run | n=500 | n=5000 | Ratio to walker (n=5000) |\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:757:evaluators scale linearly over the 10x (walker 10.1x, semantic 9.7x).\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:759:**Against the target line.** The historic register and stack VMs are deleted,\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:760:so the like-for-like reading is the ratio to the walker measured in the same\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:761:run. The historic register VM ran at 1/1.32 = 0.76x the walker and stack at\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:764:walker measures 86.96 ms against the historic v1 walker's 29.33 ms on a\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:765:different run, and the gap reflects the machine and v2's walker itself, not\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:780:| Mean time per run | Node.js (n=5000) | Dart (n=50000) | Ratio to walker |\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:788:walker, which is a slightly narrower gap than the JVM.s 0.115x. On Node the gap\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:789:is narrower: 0.278x, about 3.6x faster than the walker against 8.7x on the\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:795:now defaults to `:semantic`; `(vm :ast-walker)` still selects the walker.\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:801:[`yin.vm.divergence-register.md`](./yin.vm.divergence-register.md#the-semantic-vm-against-the-ast-walker).\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:1:(ns yin.vm.ast-walker\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:32:(declare ast-walker-restore)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:110:            {:restore-fn ast-walker-restore,\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:313:                                      {:restore-fn ast-walker-restore,\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:334:                                        {:restore-fn ast-walker-restore})]\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:342:                                        {:restore-fn ast-walker-restore})]\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:351:                  {:restore-fn ast-walker-restore,\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:463:                                                    ast-walker-restore})]\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:500:(defn- ast-walker-restore\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:501:  ([base entry] (ast-walker-restore base entry (:value entry)))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:509:  (engine/resume-from-run-queue state ast-walker-restore))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:512:(defn- ast-walker-run-active-continuation\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:754:(defn- ast-walker-run-scheduler\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:787:  (run [vm] (ffi/maybe-run vm ast-walker-run-scheduler))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:841:                       :vm-model :ast-walker))]\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:851:         (telemetry/install :ast-walker)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:524:(defn ast->datoms-with-root\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:646:(defn datoms->ast\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md | sed -n '20,90p'; nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md | sed -n '330,390p'; nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md | sed -n '640,655p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc | sed -n '500,680p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc | sed -n '150,230p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md | sed -n '20,90p'; nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md | sed -n '330,390p'; nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md | sed -n '640,655p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc | sed -n '500,680p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc | sed -n '150,230p'\"","aggregated_output":"    20\t\n    21\t**Everything is a Stream.** Code arrives on a program stream as a segment\n    22\tvalue; execution is consumption of that stream by an observer, and then\n    23\tconsumption of the segment by the machine. Effects the program performs\n    24\t(`:stream-put`, `:stream-next`, FFI) are emissions on streams the composition\n    25\tsupplied, exactly as for the walker. Optional execution traces are emissions\n    26\ttoo (§3.6). What is *not* a stream is the instruction fetch inside a loaded\n    27\tsegment, and this is deliberate: a boundary belongs \"wherever you need\n    28\tdecoupling — and nowhere else\" (`streams-all-the-way-down.md` §1). Fetch has\n    29\tone consumer, one order, no replay need, and no host boundary; putting a\n    30\tstream there is the per-value tax §4 of that note names as hopeless.\n    31\t\n    32\t**Interpretation Creates Semantics.** `[e :yin.code/op :call]` means nothing\n    33\tuntil `yin.vm.semantic` reads it. The same datoms can be read by a query\n    34\t(\"all call sites of this closure\"), a renderer (back to source through\n    35\t`:yin.code/source`), a verifier (every block ends in a terminator), or a\n    36\tdifferent VM. The mapping from the keyword mnemonic in the datom to the\n    37\tinteger opcode in the image is the loader's interpretation, not a fact in\n    38\tthe datom. `:yin.code/tail?` is a property of a call in the datoms; only the\n    39\timage decides it is a distinct `tailcall` dispatch.\n    40\t\n    41\t**Code and State are Datoms.** Instructions are entities with attributes.\n    42\tExecution state is plain data: the control is `{:segment id :pc n}`, the\n    43\tenvironment a map, the operand stack a vector, the continuation a vector of\n    44\tframes each `{:pc :env :stack-base :segment}`. The optional trace vocabulary\n    45\t(§3.6) deposits exactly these as `[e a v t m]` with `t` the step counter,\n    46\tthe \"endgame\" of `cesk-space-optimization.md` §6: execute fast, emit the\n    47\tmachine image as datoms rather than interpret from them.\n    48\t\n    49\t**Everything is a Continuation.** Park writes `{segment pc env stack k}` into\n    50\t`:parked`; resume reads it back. Nothing in that map is a host object except\n    51\tvalues the program itself put in `env` or on the stack (host functions\n    52\tresolved from primitives, stream handles from the store), which is the same\n    53\tserialization limit the walker has today. A continuation shipped through a\n    54\tstream names its segment by id; the receiving host resolves the id against\n    55\tsegment datoms it holds or was sent alongside.\n    56\t\n    57\t### 1.2 The six invariants\n    58\t\n    59\t+--------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n    60\t| Invariant                | How the design honors it                                                         |\n    61\t+--------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n    62\t| No hidden global state   | Opcode table is a value in `yin.vm`. Segments live in the VM record under     |\n    63\t|                          | `:code`. Primitives, modules, `:make-stream`, bridge handlers are all supplied   |\n    64\t|                          | at construction, as for the walker. No `defonce`, no registry.                   |\n    65\t+--------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n    66\t| No implicit control flow | Every transfer of control is an instruction datom: `:jump`, `:branch-false`,     |\n    67\t|                          | `:call`, `:return`, `:halt`. Sequencing is the explicit `:yin.code/pc` order. No |\n    68\t|                          | fallthrough that is not a `pc + 1` visible in the datoms.                        |\n    69\t+--------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n    70\t| No callbacks             | Host effects go through `engine/handle-effect` and the FFI request/response      |\n    71\t|                          | pair; a blocked read parks in the polling wait set. The VM invokes nothing and   |\n    72\t|                          | is invoked by nothing.                                                           |\n    73\t+--------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n    74\t| No shared mutable state  | The image array is built once per segment and never written after load. The hot  |\n    75\t|                          | loop keeps registers in `loop` locals; the persistent VM record is the only      |\n    76\t|                          | state that escapes a step. No mutable deftype fields (a cljd trap on record).    |\n    77\t+--------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n    78\t| No layer collapsing      | Lowering (AST datoms → code datoms), loading (code datoms → image), and          |\n    79\t|                          | execution (image → transitions) are three functions in two namespaces with data  |\n    80\t|                          | between each. The walker keeps interpreting the AST; nothing in the walker       |\n    81\t|                          | changes.                                                                         |\n    82\t+--------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n    83\t| No assumed graphs        | Branch targets and closure bodies are refs; the loader constructs the pc index   |\n    84\t|                          | from tuples in an explicit pass and resolves refs against it. A dangling ref is  |\n    85\t|                          | a load error naming the entity, never a runtime lookup.                          |\n    86\t+--------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n    87\t\n    88\t---\n    89\t\n    90\t## §2. The linear executable datom specification\n   330\t\n   331\t### 3.1 Consumption: observer, loader, image\n   332\t\n   333\tThe VM implements `IVM`; program ingress is exactly the walker's shape:\n   334\t\n   335\t- The host composes a program medium, attaches `yin.vm.stream-observer`\n   336\t  to it, and drives `observer/run-on-stream` with `engine/ready-for-ingress?`,\n   337\t  a loader, and `vm/run`. The observer owns the handle, the cursor, and gap\n   338\t  accounting. The VM holds no program stream.\n   339\t- The loader for this evaluator is `semantic/vm-load-program`: it accepts a\n   340\t  batch of `:yin.code/*` datoms, validates §2.6, builds the image, stores\n   341\t  it under `:code {segment-id image}`, and sets control to\n   342\t  `{:segment id :pc 0}`. Segment identity is stable: an id already holding\n   343\t  a different image is a load error, and an identical reload is accepted —\n   344\t  continuations name segments by id, so an id must never come to mean\n   345\t  different code. A composition that carries `:yin/*` AST datoms on\n   346\t  its program stream hands the observer `(comp semantic/vm-load-program\n   347\t  linearize/lower)` instead; the loader is composition-supplied, so no\n   348\t  evaluator learns which form travels. `yin.repl.core/make-session` is\n   349\t  where that choice is made.\n   350\t\n   351\tThe step loop is then: `(aget code pc)` → integer `case` → transition. No\n   352\thandle, no cursor, no `next` inside a segment.\n   353\t\n   354\t### 3.2 Why a branch is not a cursor move\n   355\t\n   356\tThe contract's cursor is \"a position, not a claim\" and comes only from the\n   357\tstream: minted at `:oldest`/`:newest`, returned as a successor, or recovered\n   358\tfrom a `gap`. There is no `seek`, and reaching for a transport-owned position\n   359\tanchor is forbidden for contract-generic code. Three designs were weighed:\n   360\t\n   361\t+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   362\t| Design                                                                         | Contract status                                                             | Cost                                                                             | Verdict                                                                          |\n   363\t+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   364\t| Instruction stream, `next` per fetch, branch by re-minting a block's `:oldest` | legal only if every basic block is its own stream, delivered as descriptors | one stream operation per branch, one attach per closure body, per-value boundary | rejected: it is the per-scalar boundary §4 of the streams note names as          |\n   365\t|                                                                                |                                                                             | tax on every instruction                                                         | hopeless, and a loop body would re-mint on every iteration                       |\n   366\t+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   367\t| Transport-owned position anchors as jump targets                               | illegal for contract-generic code (Surfaces)                                | —                                                                                | rejected                                                                         |\n   368\t+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   369\t| Segment as batch value; pc indexes the loaded image                            | legal; the observer already delivers batches                                | one `next` per segment                                                           | **adopted**                                                                      |\n   370\t+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   371\t\n   372\tThe cursor-as-program-counter thesis of the streams note remains true where\n   373\tit applies: an execution *trace* emitted to a stream is a straight line, and\n   374\ta debugger's cursor over it is a pc in time (§3.6).\n   375\t\n   376\t### 3.3 Read outcomes at the two boundaries\n   377\t\n   378\t*Program boundary* (observer, generic): `ok` loads and runs; `blocked` ends\n   379\tthe round with the cursor retained; `end` ends the session; `gap` commits\n   380\tthe recovery cursor and counts the loss (an evicted segment is a program\n   381\tthat never ran; the REPL latch stays host policy); `cursor-mismatch`,\n   382\t`invalid-cursor`, `transport-error` throw naming the outcome. Unchanged from\n   383\tthe divergence register.\n   384\t\n   385\t*Data boundary* (instructions, via `engine/handle-effect`): identical to the\n   386\twalker because the handlers are shared.\n   387\t\n   388\t+------------------+--------------------------------------+-------------------------------+-------------+------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------+\n   389\t| Instruction      | `ok`                                 | `blocked`/`full`              | `end`       | `gap`                                                | terminal                                                  |\n   390\t+------------------+--------------------------------------+-------------------------------+-------------+------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------+\n   640\tEvaluation order is the walker's (operator, then operands left to right),\n   641\tso effect order is identical, which the parity suite checks. Tail position\n   642\tis read from `:yin/tail?` as yang already marks it; `:if` branches inherit\n   643\tit; nothing new is inferred. Labels are resolved to instruction refs in a\n   644\tsecond pass; the linearizer emits refs, never pc integers.\n   645\t\n   646\tEvery instruction gets `:yin.code/source` = the AST node eid it came from,\n   647\tand the segment gets `:yin.code/derived-from` = the root eid. The reverse\n   648\tdirection (render an instruction range back to source) is a query, not a\n   649\tfunction in this namespace.\n   650\t\n   651\t---\n   652\t\n   653\t## §6. Performance architecture\n   654\t\n   655\t### 6.1 Traversal mechanics\n   500\t  \"Attributes with :db.cardinality/many — their values are vectors of refs\n   501\t   that must be expanded into individual :db/add assertions.\"\n   502\t  #{:yin/operands})\n   503\t\n   504\t\n   505\t(defn datoms->tx-data\n   506\t  \"Convert [e a v t m] datoms to DaoDB tx-data [:db/add e a v m].\n   507\t   Expands cardinality-many vector values into individual assertions.\n   508\t\n   509\t   DaoDB implementations like InMemoryDaoDB do not allow nil as a stored\n   510\t   value, so nil-valued assertions are omitted in this index projection.\n   511\t   The canonical datom stream remains unchanged and still carries the\n   512\t   original nil facts.\"\n   513\t  [datoms]\n   514\t  (mapcat (fn [[e a v _t m]]\n   515\t            (cond (and (contains? cardinality-many-attrs a) (vector? v))\n   516\t                  (->> v\n   517\t                       (remove nil?)\n   518\t                       (map (fn [ref] [:db/add e a ref m])))\n   519\t                  (nil? v) []\n   520\t                  :else [[:db/add e a v m]]))\n   521\t          datoms))\n   522\t\n   523\t\n   524\t(defn ast->datoms-with-root\n   525\t  \"Convert AST map into a vector of datoms. A datom is [e a v t m].\n   526\t   Returns [root-id datoms].\n   527\t\n   528\t   The last datom is always `[root-id :yin/root true t m]`, so `index-datoms`\n   529\t   finds the root without depending on emission order.\n   530\t\n   531\t   Entity IDs are tempids (negative integers: -16, -17, -18...) that get resolved\n   532\t   to actual entity IDs when transacted. The transactor assigns real positive IDs.\n   533\t\n   534\t   Options:\n   535\t     :t - transaction ID (default 0)\n   536\t     :m - metadata entity reference (default assert; see dao.datom/reserved)\n   537\t     :id-start - starting entity ID for tempids (default (- datom/first-user-id))\"\n   538\t  ([ast] (ast->datoms-with-root ast {}))\n   539\t  ([ast opts]\n   540\t   (let [id-counter (atom (or (:id-start opts) (- datom/first-user-id)))\n   541\t         t (or (:t opts) 0)\n   542\t         m (or (:m opts) datom/default-op)\n   543\t         gen-id #(swap! id-counter dec)\n   544\t         datoms (atom [])\n   545\t         emit! (fn [e attr val] (swap! datoms conj [e attr val t m]))\n   546\t         ;; Track pre-assigned EIDs already emitted so that a shared\n   547\t         ;; lambda-ast\n   548\t         ;; (same :eid in definition operand and call-site operator) is\n   549\t         ;; processed exactly once; every reference resolves to the same\n   550\t         ;; EID.\n   551\t         seen-eids (atom #{})]\n   552\t     (letfn\n   553\t       [(convert\n   554\t          [node]\n   555\t          (let [pre-eid (:eid node)\n   556\t                e (or pre-eid (gen-id))\n   557\t                {:keys [type tail?]} node]\n   558\t            (if (and pre-eid (contains? @seen-eids pre-eid))\n   559\t              ;; Shared reference already emitted — return EID without\n   560\t              ;; re-emitting.\n   561\t              e\n   562\t              (do\n   563\t                (when pre-eid (swap! seen-eids conj pre-eid))\n   564\t                (when tail? (emit! e :yin/tail? true))\n   565\t                (case type\n   566\t                  :literal (do (emit! e :yin/type :literal)\n   567\t                               (emit! e :yin/value (:value node)))\n   568\t                  :variable (do (emit! e :yin/type :variable)\n   569\t                                (emit! e :yin/name (:name node)))\n   570\t                  :lambda (do (emit! e :yin/type :lambda)\n   571\t                              (when (:macro? node)\n   572\t                                (emit! e :yin/macro? true))\n   573\t                              (emit! e :yin/params (:params node))\n   574\t                              (let [body-id (convert (:body node))]\n   575\t                                (emit! e :yin/body body-id)))\n   576\t                  :application (do (emit! e :yin/type :application)\n   577\t                                   (let [op-id (convert (:operator node))\n   578\t                                         operand-ids (mapv convert\n   579\t                                                           (:operands node))]\n   580\t                                     (emit! e :yin/operator op-id)\n   581\t                                     (emit! e :yin/operands operand-ids)))\n   582\t                  :dao.stream.apply/call\n   583\t                  (do (emit! e :yin/type :dao.stream.apply/call)\n   584\t                      (emit! e :yin/op (:op node))\n   585\t                      (let [operand-ids (mapv convert (:operands node))]\n   586\t                        (emit! e :yin/operands operand-ids)))\n   587\t                  :if (do (emit! e :yin/type :if)\n   588\t                          (let [test-id (convert (:test node))\n   589\t                                cons-id (convert (:consequent node))\n   590\t                                alt-id (convert (:alternate node))]\n   591\t                            (emit! e :yin/test test-id)\n   592\t                            (emit! e :yin/consequent cons-id)\n   593\t                            (emit! e :yin/alternate alt-id)))\n   594\t                  ;; VM primitives\n   595\t                  :vm/gensym (do (emit! e :yin/type :vm/gensym)\n   596\t                                 (emit! e :yin/prefix (or (:prefix node) \"id\")))\n   597\t                  :vm/store-get (do (emit! e :yin/type :vm/store-get)\n   598\t                                    (emit! e :yin/key (:key node)))\n   599\t                  :vm/store-put (do (emit! e :yin/type :vm/store-put)\n   600\t                                    (emit! e :yin/key (:key node))\n   601\t                                    (emit! e :yin/value (:val node)))\n   602\t                  ;; Stream operations\n   603\t                  :stream/make (do\n   604\t                                 (emit! e :yin/type :stream/make)\n   605\t                                 (emit! e :yin/buffer (or (:buffer node) 1024)))\n   606\t                  :stream/put (do (emit! e :yin/type :stream/put)\n   607\t                                  (let [target-id (convert (:target node))\n   608\t                                        val-id (convert (:val node))]\n   609\t                                    (emit! e :yin/target target-id)\n   610\t                                    (emit! e :yin/val-node val-id)))\n   611\t                  :stream/cursor (do (emit! e :yin/type :stream/cursor)\n   612\t                                     (let [source-id (convert (:source node))]\n   613\t                                       (emit! e :yin/source source-id)))\n   614\t                  :stream/next (do (emit! e :yin/type :stream/next)\n   615\t                                   (let [source-id (convert (:source node))]\n   616\t                                     (emit! e :yin/source source-id)))\n   617\t                  :stream/close (do (emit! e :yin/type :stream/close)\n   618\t                                    (let [source-id (convert (:source node))]\n   619\t                                      (emit! e :yin/source source-id)))\n   620\t                  ;; Continuation primitives\n   621\t                  :vm/park (emit! e :yin/type :vm/park)\n   622\t                  :vm/resume (do (emit! e :yin/type :vm/resume)\n   623\t                                 (emit! e :yin/parked-id (:parked-id node))\n   624\t                                 (let [val-id (convert (:val node))]\n   625\t                                   (emit! e :yin/val-node val-id)))\n   626\t                  :vm/current-continuation\n   627\t                  (emit! e :yin/type :vm/current-continuation)\n   628\t                  ;; Default\n   629\t                  (throw (ex-info \"Unknown AST node type\"\n   630\t                                  {:type type, :node node})))\n   631\t                e))))]\n   632\t       (let [root-id (convert ast)]\n   633\t         (emit! root-id :yin/root true)\n   634\t         [root-id @datoms])))))\n   635\t\n   636\t\n   637\t(defn ast->datoms\n   638\t  \"Convert AST map into a vector of datoms. A datom is [e a v t m].\"\n   639\t  ([ast] (ast->datoms ast {}))\n   640\t  ([ast opts] (second (ast->datoms-with-root ast opts))))\n   641\t\n   642\t\n   643\t(declare index-datoms)\n   644\t\n   645\t\n   646\t(defn datoms->ast\n   647\t  \"Convert a vector of datoms [e a v t m] back into an AST map.\"\n   648\t  ([datoms]\n   649\t   (let [indexed (index-datoms datoms)\n   650\t         root-id (:root-id indexed)]\n   651\t     (datoms->ast datoms {:indexed indexed, :root-id root-id})))\n   652\t  ([datoms {:keys [indexed root-id]}]\n   653\t   (let [{:keys [get-attr]} indexed\n   654\t         node-type (get-attr root-id :yin/type)\n   655\t         tail? (get-attr root-id :yin/tail?)\n   656\t         base (cond-> {:type node-type} tail? (assoc :tail? true))\n   657\t         recur-ast (fn [eid]\n   658\t                     (datoms->ast datoms {:indexed indexed, :root-id eid}))]\n   659\t     (case node-type\n   660\t       :literal (assoc base :value (get-attr root-id :yin/value))\n   661\t       :variable (assoc base :name (get-attr root-id :yin/name))\n   662\t       :lambda (let [body-eid (get-attr root-id :yin/body)\n   663\t                     macro? (get-attr root-id :yin/macro?)]\n   664\t                 (cond-> (assoc base\n   665\t                                :params (get-attr root-id :yin/params)\n   666\t                                :body (recur-ast body-eid))\n   667\t                   macro? (assoc :macro? true)))\n   668\t       :application (let [op-eid (get-attr root-id :yin/operator)\n   669\t                          operand-eids (get-attr root-id :yin/operands)]\n   670\t                      ;; §2.4 saturation, applied by the loader: the codec\n   671\t                      ;; emits only `true`, so an application written without\n   672\t                      ;; the mark and one stating :tail? false are one value,\n   673\t                      ;; and this loader and the row loader (§7.2 part 2)\n   674\t                      ;; yield the same :program\n   675\t                      (assoc base\n   676\t                             :tail? (boolean tail?)\n   677\t                             :operator (recur-ast op-eid)\n   678\t                             :operands (mapv recur-ast operand-eids)))\n   679\t       :dao.stream.apply/call (let [op (get-attr root-id :yin/op)\n   680\t                                    operand-eids (get-attr root-id\n   150\t          (recur (inc i))))\n   151\t      [@code @labels])))\n   152\t\n   153\t\n   154\t(defn lower\n   155\t  \"Lower one AST program, as `[e a v t m]` datoms, to one code segment.\n   156\t\n   157\t   Options:\n   158\t     :t        transaction id (default 0)\n   159\t     :id-start the segment tempid; instruction pc n is `id-start - 1 - n`.\n   160\t               Defaults to below every entity id in the input, so the code\n   161\t               and its AST can travel in one batch without colliding.\n   162\t\n   163\t   Throws naming the node on an unsupported, unknown, or missing node type,\n   164\t   or on a host value (a function or object) in a data operand or its\n   165\t   metadata. Throws on a supplied :t that is not an integer, and on an\n   166\t   :id-start that is not a negative integer, whose range leaves the\n   167\t   host-safe integers, or whose range collides with an input entity.\"\n   168\t  ([datoms] (lower datoms {}))\n   169\t  ([datoms opts]\n   170\t   (let [datoms (vec datoms)\n   171\t         {:keys [get-attr root-id error]} (vm/index-datoms datoms)\n   172\t         _ (when error\n   173\t             (throw (ex-info \"Cannot lower a program with a dangling root\" error)))\n   174\t         _ (when (nil? root-id)\n   175\t             (throw (ex-info \"Cannot lower a program with no root\" {})))\n   176\t         [code labels] (flatten-program get-attr root-id)\n   177\t         input-ids (set (map first datoms))\n   178\t         ;; A supplied option is validated as given, even when falsey.\n   179\t         seg (if (contains? opts :id-start)\n   180\t               (:id-start opts)\n   181\t               (dec (reduce min (- datom/first-user-id) input-ids)))\n   182\t         _ (when-not (and (integer? seg) (neg? seg))\n   183\t             (throw (ex-info \"Cannot lower with an :id-start that is not a negative integer\"\n   184\t                             {:id-start seg})))\n   185\t         ;; pcs allocate seg-1 .. seg-length, below seg and so all negative.\n   186\t         ;; They are distinct on every host only while the whole range stays\n   187\t         ;; within min-safe-id; the sum is exact because both terms are safe.\n   188\t         _ (when (< seg (+ min-safe-id (count code)))\n   189\t             (throw (ex-info \"Cannot lower with code tempids below the host-safe integer range\"\n   190\t                             {:id-start seg, :length (count code),\n   191\t                              :min-safe-id min-safe-id})))\n   192\t         last-id (- seg (count code))\n   193\t         collisions (sort (filter #(and (number? %) (<= last-id % seg)) input-ids))\n   194\t         _ (when (seq collisions)\n   195\t             (throw (ex-info \"Cannot lower with code tempids colliding with input entities\"\n   196\t                             {:id-start seg, :last-id last-id, :collisions (vec collisions)})))\n   197\t         pc->eid #(- seg 1 %)\n   198\t         t (get opts :t 0)\n   199\t         _ (when-not (integer? t)\n   200\t             (throw (ex-info \"Cannot lower with a :t that is not an integer\" {:t t})))\n   201\t         m datom/default-op]\n   202\t     (into [[seg :yin.code/type :segment t m]\n   203\t            [seg :yin.code/length (count code) t m]\n   204\t            [seg :yin.code/derived-from root-id t m]]\n   205\t           (mapcat (fn [pc {:keys [op source operands]}]\n   206\t                     (let [e (pc->eid pc)]\n   207\t                       (concat [[e :yin.code/segment seg t m]\n   208\t                                [e :yin.code/pc pc t m]\n   209\t                                [e :yin.code/op op t m]]\n   210\t                               (map (fn [[a v]]\n   211\t                                      [e a\n   212\t                                       (if (contains? label-attrs a)\n   213\t                                         (pc->eid (get labels v))\n   214\t                                         v)\n   215\t                                       t m])\n   216\t                                    operands)\n   217\t                               [[e :yin.code/source source t m]])))\n   218\t                   (range)\n   219\t                   code)))))\n   220\t\n   221\t\n   222\t(defn- row-slots\n   223\t  \"The slot values of row `id` in §2.3 table order: its body minus the tag.\"\n   224\t  [rows id]\n   225\t  (subvec (get rows id) 2))\n   226\t\n   227\t\n   228\t(defn- flatten-rows\n   229\t  \"The §5.3 flattening from `root` over rows, the row-lane twin of\n   230\t   `flatten-program`: the same recursion in the walker's evaluation order,\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
